course: dictionary
lesson: 8925
title: Reading feelings in a sigh
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sigh is a long, slow breath, let out loudly. People sigh when they are tired, sad, bored or relieved, so a sigh tells you how a character feels. «Picture a kitchen in Leeds at seven o'clock: Mum is answering an email with one hand and stirring the pasta with the other, sighing about "another meeting that could have been an email".» Mum is busy and fed up, so her sigh shows she is tired of meetings.
explain: Look at what happens just before and after the sigh. In «Gareth makes Leon pancakes every Saturday and sits through his fractions without sighing», the word without tells you Gareth does not sigh, so he is patient and calm. Writers use sighs to show feelings without having to say them.
explain: To check your idea, try your meaning in the sentence. If Mum is fed up, does «sighing about another meeting» make sense? Yes, so you have worked out the feeling.
fact: To sigh means to breathe out slowly and loudly.
fact: A sigh is that long, loud breath.
fact: People sigh when they are tired, sad, bored or relieved.
fact: «She sighed with relief» means she felt glad that something bad was over.
fact: The words around a sigh, such as «without sighing», give you clues about how a character feels.
exercise: «Mum signed it, sighed, and they made a homework plan.» Find and copy the word that means breathed out loudly. | plan | homework | signed | *sighed
exercise: «Gareth makes Leon pancakes every Saturday and sits through his fractions without sighing.» What does the word without tell you about Gareth? | He is asleep | He is bored and sighs loudly all lesson | He is angry and shouts | *He stays patient and calm, not fed up
exercise: What is a sigh? | A loud noise made by a door | A type of cold drink | A bird that sings at dawn | *A long, loud breath let out slowly
test: «She sighed with relief when the test was over.» How does she feel? | *Glad that it is over | Bored | Angry | Hungry
test: Which phrase shows someone feeling relieved? | a cup of tea | a loud shout | a pair of shoes | *a sigh of relief
exam: «Then he heaves a long, long sigh» in a story: what should the reader think? | The character is cold and needs a coat | *The character is tired, sad or fed up, depending on the words around it | The character is singing | The character has just won a prize
